text:
Alex K: quick question about a 'lazy-commit' approach for host-backed pages: instead of pinning/copying at alloc time, only materialize GPU mappings when the kernel actually touches the page. could save a lot for cold slices but worried about metadata bloat and correctness w/ async kernels. thoughts?

Jess P: sounds useful for startup and transient workloads. how do you detect first touch without huge stalls? page-fault handler on the GPU side is risky. we have hostfault shim but last-mile latency matters. :thinking_face:

Liam Z: you can do lightweight first-touch handling: queue a commit task on fault and continue other work; only block when the kernel needs that specific tile. problem is some kernels expect contiguous mapping and will busy-wait.

Maria S: right — we need explicit commit fences. also need compact per-allocation metadata. proposing chunk-level commit records (e.g., 64KB chunks) with an epoch + flags (committed, dirty). that gives O(#chunks) state, not O(#pages).

Alex K: sketch idea: commit record per chunk, epoch increments on commit; commit operation sets committed flag and issues a runtime fence. kernel pagefault path enqueues commit and returns a transient fault to retry. only when retry still faults do we block. minimizes blocking in common case.

Jess P: i like chunking. ensures we don't explode with tiny allocations. two follow-ups: 1) who reclaims commit records? 2) how do we prevent tenant memory amplification from malicious clients?

Liam Z: reclaim on eviction or when chunk unused for N epochs. tenant quota needs to account for 'potential commits' (allocated but not committed) + committed residency. soft-accounting should trigger early GC.

Maria S: also consider writeback semantics: if a committed chunk is dirtied on GPU and evicted, we must either writeback to host backing store or persist dirty-gen so next materialization can replay. simplest: always writeback on eviction; heavier but correct. alternative: keep redo logs per-chunk (smaller) and replay on remap.

Alex K: tradeoff matrix we discussed offline: writeback-on-evict (simple, bandwidth heavy) vs redo-log (complex, metadata heavy). redo-log could be limited to large tensors only.

Jess P: operational concerns: need telemetry for commit rate, commit latency, deferred-fault count, redo-log size. add counters + perf-test.

ops-bot: perf-run queued: lazy-commit/epoch-proto branch -> perf-suite/host-paging loaded. CI link: https://ci.internal/runs/6421 :robot_face:

Alex K: i'll draft an RFC with pseudocode and TTL heuristics. attaching tiny pseudo:```c
// commit record per 64KB chunk
struct commit_rec { uint32_t epoch; uint8_t flags; /* bit0=committed, bit1=dirty */ };

void commit_chunk(chunk_id c) {
  if (rec[c].flags & COMMITTED) return;
  rec[c].epoch = next_epoch();
  rec[c].flags |= COMMITTED;
  runtime_submit_commit_fence(c);
}

// fault path (GPU-facing)
on_gpu_fault(addr) {
  chunk = addr_to_chunk(addr);
  if (!(rec[chunk].flags & COMMITTED)) {
    enqueue_commit_task(chunk);
    return SHORT_FAULT_RETRY; // user-space/kernel will retry
  }
  // if we reach here and still fault, block and escalate
  block_until_committed_or_fail(chunk);
}
```

Liam Z: looks good. in RFC add cost thresholds: precommit if request.batch_size > 8 || tail_latency target < 50ms. also a config knob for chunk_size.

Maria S: also include a tenant-protection doc: max commit-records per-tenant, fallback to eager pinning when quota reached.

Alex K: great, will send RFC by EOD and link perf numbers. thanks all. :heart:
